22:26:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I totally get why you’d say that—it’s exactly what many of us first learn: the periodic table is a neat chart of the elements found in nature. And for the first 92 elements (up to uranium), that’s mostly true. But the full picture is a bit more surprising. If you look closely at any modern periodic table, you’ll see a bunch of elements with an asterisk or a little icon next to them—those are **synthetic**, meaning they don’t exist naturally on Earth in any measurable amount. They were literally “cooked up” in machines, and particle accelerators are the main kitchen. ### Just a few famous examples - **Technetium (43)**: There’s a gap in the table right below manganese. It wasn’t found in nature; it was first created in a cyclotron in 1937. All its isotopes are radioactive, and the tiny amounts that briefly occur in uranium ore come from fission, not primordial Earth. - **Plutonium (94)**: Beyond uranium, all the elements we know of are man-made. Plutonium was first synthesized in a cyclotron in 1940, then later found in trace quantities in nature—but those traces came from natural nuclear reactions, not from original formation of the Earth. - **Americium (95)**: The stuff that makes your smoke detector work? It’s manufactured in nuclear reactors. No natural source. - **Elements 95 through 118**: Every one of them is synthetic. Many were created by slamming lighter nuclei together in particle accelerators. Oganesson (118), for instance, was made by firing a beam of calcium-48 at a californium target—only a handful of atoms ever existed. ### Why does “basic science class” give a different impression? Early science classes often present a simplified periodic table and focus on the naturally occurring elements because they’re the ones with tangible examples—iron, oxygen, gold, etc. It’s easier to talk about things you can actually see. The fact that we’ve expanded the table using accelerators gets introduced later, in more advanced chemistry or physics. But the discovery of these new elements is a standard, well-documented part of modern science. The official boundaries of the periodic table are now determined by the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC), and each new element comes from a specific experiment at a lab like GSI in Germany, RIKEN in Japan, or JINR in Russia—all using particle accelerators. So yes, the basic idea that there’s a fixed set of natural elements is a useful starting point. But the real periodic table has a whole section that only exists because we figured out how to create new nuclei. Far from being unscientific, it’s one of the triumphs of modern science that we can, in fact, cook up elements that nature never bothered to make on Earth.
